--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertCalled` reads the record a double kept, by the method's name alone, by an exact count and by the arguments one call was made with — `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0079 § 11: the calls are asserted *after* the exercise, against the
// record the double kept, in the order the test reads. Nothing is declared in
// advance, so every line below is about a call that has already happened.
interface Mailer {
    public function send(string $to, string $subject): bool;
    public function purge(): void;
}

Mailer $mailer = Core\Test::double<Mailer>({
    send: fn(string $to, string $subject): bool => true,
    purge: fn(): void => {},
});

// The exercise. Two calls to one method, with different arguments, so every
// question below has more than one answer available to it.
echo $mailer->send("ada@example.test", "welcome") ? "sent" : "refused", "\n";
echo $mailer->send("bob@example.test", "reminder") ? "sent" : "refused", "\n";

// 1. The name alone: it was called, and how often is not being asked.
Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::send);
echo "called\n";

// 2. `times` is exact rather than a floor — two calls are two, and one is a
// failure even though the method was called.
Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::send, {times: 2});
Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
    Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::send, {times: 1});
});
echo "twice, and not once\n";

// 3. `with` names the arguments of one recorded call, all of them and in
// order: a list that matches a prefix of a call matches no call at all.
Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::send, {with: ["bob@example.test", "reminder"]});
Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
    Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::send, {with: ["bob@example.test"]});
});
echo "with the arguments of one call\n";

// 4. The two narrow together, which is what makes the pair worth having: of
// the two calls recorded, exactly one was made with these arguments.
Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::send, {times: 1, with: ["ada@example.test", "welcome"]});
echo "one call carried these arguments\n";

// 5. A method the exercise never reached has no record at all, and asking
// about it is a failure rather than an error — the ledger holds no key for a
// method that was never called.
Core\Test::expectFailure(fn (): void => {
    Core\Test::assertCalled($mailer, Mailer::purge);
});
echo "an uncalled method fails\n";
--EXPECT--
sent
sent
called
twice, and not once
with the arguments of one call
one call carried these arguments
an uncalled method fails
